data: {"type":"response.completed","response":{"id":"resp_315823a6db7949a9a1094cd7b40078a6","object":"response","created_at":1792915755,"status":"completed","background":false,"completed_at":1792915764,"max_output_tokens":2048,"model":"LongCat-2.5-Preview","output":[{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_resp_315823a6db7949a9a1094cd7b40078a6","summary":[],"content":[{"type":"reasoning_text","text":"\nWe need n divisible by 12, with exactly 12 divisors. Find smallest.\n\nn divisible by 12 = 2²·3. So n = 2^a · 3^b · (other primes), a ≥ 2, b ≥ 1.\n\nDivisor count: (a+1)(b+1)... = 12.\n\nPossible exponent patterns for 12 divisors: 12 = 12 → p^11; 6·2 → p^5 q; 4·3 → p^3 q^2; 3·2·2 → p^2 q r.\n\nSince n must have 2^a with a≥2 and 3^b with b≥1:\n- p^11: need 2^11, but then 3 doesn't divide. No.\n- p^5 q: exponents 5 and 1. Need a≥2, b≥1: a=5, b=1 → 2^5·3 = 96. Check: divisors = 6·2=12. 96 divisible by 12? 96/12=8 yes. So 96 works.\n- p^3 q^2: exponents 3, 2. a must be ≥2, b≥1. Options: a=3, b=2 → 2^3·3^2 = 72. Divisors: 4·3=12. 72/12 = 6. Yes! 72 works.\n- p^2 q r: exponents 2,1,1. a=2, then 3^1 and another prime ≥5: 2^2·3·5 = 60. Divisors: 3·2·2 = 12. 60 divisible by 12? Yes. 60 < 72.\n\nSo smallest is 60.\n\nCheck smaller multiples of 12: 12 (6 divisors), 24 (8), 36 (9), 48 (10), 60 (12). Yes, 60.\n"}]},{"type":"message","id":"msg_d301b38985914d2089af1f5d4e5b2701","status":"completed","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"**60**\n\nn = 2²·3·5 = 60 gives (2+1)(1+1)(1+1) = 12 divisors, and 60/12 = 5. Checking smaller multiples of 12: 12→6 divisors, 24→8, 36→9, 48→10. So 60 is the smallest.\n\n\\boxed{60}","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}]}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"reasoning":{"effort":"high","summary":"concise"},"service_tier":"default","store":true,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[],"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":43,"input_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":569,"output_tok